 trend  reversal here it's a bit like a bathtub  you know if you have to tap running the  bathtub the levels will still rise the  only way to actually turn things around  is to switch off the bathtub  switch off the tap only that  carbon-neutral economy and society will  get us where we need to be with  countries desperate to get their  economies going after this corona virus  pandemic isn't there a danger that  fighting climate change will be off the  agenda for years to come  well there certainly is that risk  because yeah the economic crisis were  facing the global recession that we're  in for millions of crops at stake  also the pandemic these are very  imminent threats for more concrete and  tangible to many people then climate  change but climate change let's not  forget is by far the greater threat is  an existential threat for mankind so we  have to get it on the agenda and I think  actually we have a window of opportunity  here because we have leverage with the  bailouts you know all these bailouts  that'll be drawn up to prop up the  system prop up companies we should use  this opportunity to actually extract  concessions from the companies who tap  into this money it's taxpayers money  it's your money it's my money and we  should get binding commitments that puts  them on a more sustainable track so  there might be some potential there but  the lockdown has already radically  changed human behavior patterns do you  think these changes could have an  impulsive implications for the  environment well yeah we've seen a lot  of change these past months with the  lockdown and unfortunately I'm afraid  that most of them probably won't be  lasting there a lot of people out there  who want to get back to the old status  quo rather sooner than later but there's  one aspect regarding mobility where I  think we have a chance to have an impact  on the environment and that is home  office we've had quite a few ability  study undertaken here these past few  months you know with the lockdown we've  managed to swing it even under very  adverse conditions even with home  schooling we've pulled off home office  in many sectors and if you think about  it that transport accounts for about 30%  of all co2 emissions in the EU and  three-quarters of that is down to road  traffic so if we could take commuters  off the roads by giving them more home  office possibilities they could actually  make a huge dent in  those co2 emissions and it would also  enhance work/life balance for employees  and happy employees are good employees  so I think if we made home office the  new normal  rather than the exception that would be  a great takeaway from this crisis Neal  thank you so much that was Neal King  there from our environmental desk  thank you even though co2 emissions have  slowed worldwide due to coronavirus  restrictions environmentalists fear a  spike in waste from protective equipment  which often ends up in the world's  oceans and seas the air is clean in  Paris with most airline flights grounded  and many people stuck at home  but the see is another story here off  the Mediterranean coast of France masks  and gloves are plentiful this is the  first sign of an advanced type of  pollution if nothing is done a beautiful  coast we know that as soon as it starts  to rain all the rubbish coming from the  gutters will end up in the sea and we  could see more in the weeks to come  plastic personal protective equipment or  PPE has helped millions of people to  deal with the pandemic it's allowed  businesses to reopen and travel to  resume and many people are reluctant to  give it up like here in Italy everybody  being very careful all of us travel with  our kid I'm in favor of face masks  I wear a double mask health authorities  around the world warned against reusing  plastic PPE the risk of infection is too  high meanwhile researchers are  experimenting with disinfecting masks  with UV light widespread masks use is  expected to continue for years to come  billions and billions more of these are  likely to end up in the ocean unless  people figure out a better way to stay  safe
 

